---
import { IS_TWITCH_ONLY } from "../../lib/constants";
---

<h2>1. Overview</h2>
<p>
    Mixchat is designed to function without a Mixchat-operated database for
    chat data. Account credentials live in <code>httpOnly</code> cookies, not
    in a server account store. Chat history is cached on your device. Logging
    out removes auth cookies and the UI session; local caches described below
    may remain until you delete them.
</p>
<p>
    A few opt-in streamer tools write small JSON files under the local app
    data directory (Song Queue, Wheel Spin, and Stream tools
    settings/state). None of these store chat transcripts or login
    credentials. YouTube live chat is also relayed through the local server
    process in memory while you watch. See sections 2.2, 3.1, and 4 below.
</p>

<h2>2. Data Collection</h2>

<h3>
    2.1 Logging In{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? " (Twitch)" : " (Twitch & YouTube)"}
</h3>
<p>
    When connecting a Twitch{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "" : " or YouTube"} account, the
    app gets "keys" (OAuth tokens) from{" "}
    {IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "the service" : "those services"}.
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Secure Storage:</strong> Key tokens are stored as{" "}
        <code>httpOnly</code> cookies. This helps block outside scripts from
        seeing them.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>UI Session:</strong> A little bit of info (like your username
        and profile picture) is saved in <code>localStorage</code> so the app
        remembers who you are on refresh. The access token is stripped before
        saving; a provider refresh token may remain in that stored session.
    </li>
</ul>
<p>
    In the desktop app those cookies and local storage live in the app webview
    profile on your machine (see section 3.1). Sign-in flows differ from the
    website:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Twitch:</strong> Device Code Grant. The activation page may
        open in an in-app webview that shares the same profile, so Twitch
        cookies used by the embedded player stay on your device.
    </li>
    {!IS_TWITCH_ONLY && (
        <li>
            <strong>YouTube/Google:</strong> Sign-in opens in your system
            browser. A short-lived local handoff record (up to 10 minutes,
            single use) returns the result to the app. The system browser never
            receives Mixchat auth cookies.
        </li>
    )}
</ul>

<h3>2.2 Your Messages</h3>
<p>
    Twitch chat reaches the app over Twitch's own connections and is rendered
    from memory (with an optional local scrollback cache). YouTube live chat
    is proxied through the local Mixchat server process, which holds a
    short-lived in-memory copy (up to about 300 messages per open session,
    reaped after idle time). Recent messages may also be cached on your device
    in IndexedDB for up to 5 days so you can scroll back; that cache is
    pruned automatically and is not uploaded.
</p>

{!IS_TWITCH_ONLY && (
    <>
        <h3>2.3 YouTube Integration</h3>
        <p>
            By linking a YouTube channel, Mixchat uses YouTube APIs with your
            sign-in to show live status and stream metadata, read and send
            live chat messages, and carry out moderation actions you trigger
            (delete a message, ban a viewer). Live chat delivery uses
            YouTube's internal (Innertube) endpoints in addition to the
            official Data API.
        </p>
    </>
)}

<h2>3. Third-Party Services</h2>
<p>To make everything work smoothly, Mixchat talks to a few core services:</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Twitch{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "" : " & YouTube"}:</strong> For the
        chat and video feeds.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Emote Providers (7TV, BTTV, FFZ):</strong> To render those
        custom emotes everyone loves. These services might see which channel
        you're watching so they can serve the right emote pack.
    </li>
    {!IS_TWITCH_ONLY && (
        <li>
            <strong>Google Drive (optional):</strong> If you enable settings
            backup, a JSON file of your settings can be saved to and restored
            from your own Google Drive. This is user-initiated and separate
            from chat. The backup can include stream-tool history keys such as
            past playlists (which may hold Song Queue owner tokens) and
            giveaway winner history.
        </li>
    )}
</ul>
<p>
    Other third parties the app may contact when you use it: a Twitch recent-
    message history relay (<code>recent-messages.robotty.de</code>), Google
    Fonts, Twemoji/Giphy CDNs, and embedded Twitch/YouTube players.
</p>
<p>Mixchat does not include analytics or telemetry.</p>

<h2>3.1 Desktop App Data</h2>
<p>
    The desktop app runs Mixchat as a local Node process bound only to{" "}
    <code>127.0.0.1</code>. There is no Mixchat cloud database for chat. What
    stays on your machine:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Login cookies and webview profile</strong> (including any
        IndexedDB caches): Windows{" "}
        <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\xyz.grepfs.mixchat</code>; Linux under the XDG
        data directory for <code>xyz.grepfs.mixchat</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Settings and local tool data:</strong> installed builds use
        the OS app data directory; portable builds use the{" "}
        <code>app-data</code> folder next to the executable (cookies still
        live in the profile path above).
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Logs:</strong> sidecar output is written locally with secrets
        redacted. Logs are not uploaded.
    </li>
</ul>
<p>
    Deleting those folders removes local Mixchat data. Uninstalling the app
    alone may leave them in place (on Windows, tick "Delete app data" in the
    uninstaller if offered). Platform providers (Twitch, YouTube, emote CDNs)
    still receive the usual requests when the app is online.
</p>

<h2>4. Stateless and Database-Free</h2>
<p>
    Mixchat's server/API is "stateless" for chat and OAuth data. This means it
    doesn't remember sessions from one second to the next without the client
    sending its security key. There is no Mixchat backend database for
    messages, accounts, or emote data.
</p>
<p>
    The local desktop server only holds short-lived state such as the Google
    sign-in handoff described in section 2.1, plus opt-in local tool files
    under the app data directory (Song Queue, Wheel, Stream tools). Ephemeral
    in-memory state also exists while open (for example YouTube chat fan-out
    and poll command relay).
</p>

<h2>5. Data Retention and Deletion</h2>
<p>
    <strong>Logout</strong> clears auth cookies and the UI session on this
    device. It does not by itself delete IndexedDB caches (such as the 5-day
    chat history) or other local settings.
</p>
<p>
    For a full local wipe in the desktop app, quit the app and delete the app
    data directory and webview profile listed in section 3.1 (portable: also
    remove the <code>app-data</code> folder next to the executable).
</p>

<h2>6. Security</h2>
<p>
    Login tokens are managed using secure HTTP-only cookies. Desktop builds
    use public OAuth clients (no hosted-site secrets in the binary). It is
    recommended to use strong passwords and two-factor authentication (2FA)
    on Twitch{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "" : " and YouTube"} accounts.
</p>
